Filter pill bar for the beamenu launcher: one capsule per provider that has
rows, drawn between the search row and the list in the rich layout.

Narrow FFI, mirroring the shape of the rest of the rich-mode surface:
bm_menu_set_pills() takes a `\x1f`-separated `label:count` spec plus an
active index, and bm_menu_get_active_pill() reads back whatever Tab and
Shift+Tab last moved it to. Cycling happens inside bm_menu_run_with_key(),
ahead of the vim-binding switch and the rest of the key handling, so it
replaces rather than falls through to Tab's stock highlight-next and
Shift+Tab's stock copy-to-filter for as long as a pill bar is set. The
client sets and polls the pill bar every frame; the library owns the
keystroke in between.

The client decides which providers have rows, so the spec it sends shrinks
and grows between frames, and an empty spec clears the bar outright. Two
consequences worth naming. The row's height already collapses to 0 when
pill_count is 0, so the panel reflows on its own. And patch 02 draws its
in-list section headings again whenever the bar is empty, which is now what
a keyword query (`=2+2`, sectioned "Calculator") or a query matching no
ambient row looks like. That patch's header calls the bar "always-visible",
which stopped being true here rather than there.

An active index of BM_PILL_NONE (UINT32_MAX) means nothing is filtering. The
bar still draws, and the capsule naming the highlighted row's section takes
the active fill instead of its outline, since no chosen capsule is left to
confuse it with. A launcher wants this while a search spans every section:
the bar then reports where the highlight landed rather than what is
filtering. The sentinel survives bm_menu_set_pills()'s clamp deliberately,
and stepping off it with Tab/Shift+Tab is spelled out rather than left to
unsigned wraparound. Forwards happens to land on 0 by accident; backwards
would give UINT32_MAX - 1, an index that is neither the sentinel nor a real
pill, and every later Tab would walk on from there.

The run scrolls instead of clipping. Capsules that did not fit were dropped
without a trace, which hid whichever providers sorted last: six capsules
against a 720px panel meant the sixth simply never appeared. The run is now
offset so the focus capsule stays on screen (the active one, else the
current one, else the first), and drawing is clipped to the row so a
partly-visible capsule is cut cleanly at the edge instead of vanishing.
Centring that capsule rather than scrolling by the smallest amount that fits
is deliberate: the minimum shift pins it against the right edge on the way
out, then jumps the whole bar back to 0 as soon as Shift+Tab brings it
inside again, which reads as a glitch. Centring moves about one capsule per
Tab in either direction.

The offset is the one piece of this patch that is pure arithmetic, with no
cairo context, no pango measurement and no menu state, so it lives in its
own translation unit: lib/renderers/pills.cpp, reached through extern "C".
It uses no STL, exceptions or RTTI, so the archive links into the C
renderers without dragging in libstdc++. Being pure also makes it the piece
worth testing on its own. nix/patches/beamenu/tests/pills_scroll_test.cpp in
the dots repo covers it under -fsanitize=address,undefined, and stays
outside this patch so the series has one less hunk to rebase.

The row's height joins the same total_height math patch 05 introduced for
the rich panel, and shifts the list's start (posy) down by exactly that
much, the same way patch 03's search row already does. It is otherwise
additive: no line patch 01-05 touched is modified, only new functions and a
handful of small, clearly separated hunks that read values those patches
already compute (ascii_height, search_h's sibling total_height branch,
titleh). Pill capsules reuse bm_cairo_rounded_path from patch 02 for their
fully-rounded shape, and the capsule height is derived from the item font's
own ascii_height rather than from line_height, so it does not depend on
whatever row height patch 02 ends up configured with.

The count run needs a smaller point size than anything else this file draws
(which is otherwise always at the configured item font size), so this patch
adds bm_pango_get_layout_sized/bm_cairo_run_width_sized/
bm_cairo_draw_run_sized as siblings of the existing unsized helpers rather
than changing their signatures.

Layout values the design left unspecified (internal pill padding, the gaps
between a pill's dot/label/count) were chosen to read as a scaled-down
version of the row gutters bm_cairo_paint_rich_rows already uses (pad_x/
gutter 10px there becomes 12px/6px here); see the comment above the
BM_PILL_* constants in cairo_renderer.h.

bm_pills_free() also resets pill_active to 0. bm_menu_get_active_pill()'s
contract is "0 when no pill bar is set", and bm_menu_set_pills(menu, "", ...)
used to leave pill_active exactly where Tab/Shift+Tab last left it instead of
honouring that; bm_menu_free() now shares bm_pills_free() (forward declared
next to it) rather than duplicating its loop, which the same read caught.

bm_cairo_paint_pills_row() also marks whichever pill names the currently
highlighted row's section as "current", replacing the in-list section
headings patch 02 stops drawing whenever this bar is present (see that
patch's header). No new FFI: bm_menu_get_highlighted_item() and
bm_item_get_section() are both already public, so the match is a plain
string compare between the highlighted item's section and each pill's label,
the same strings bm_menu_set_pills()'s spec was built from in the first
place, done once per paint rather than per pill. Active beats current when a
pill is both, so the filled active state is never doubled up with the
current outline. The current pill keeps the transparent background of a
plain pill but strokes its border in BM_COLOR_HIGHLIGHTED_BG instead of
BM_COLOR_BORDER, adds a second concentric rounded stroke ~2px further out at
20% of that colour's alpha as a focus ring, and brightens its label/count
from BM_COLOR_ALTERNATE_FG to BM_COLOR_ITEM_FG: the same three-tier
treatment (fill / accent outline+ring / plain outline) a focus-visible chip
gets in the design, adapted to what cairo_stroke can do with two paths
instead of a CSS box-shadow.

